 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Sorting Bottleneck:** The primary bottleneck is sorting millions of gaussians, which is done efficiently in the original implementation using CUB device radix sort, a highly optimized sort only available in CUDA [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Precision:** Degenerate ellipsoids (zero determinant) can cause numerical instability in inversion. The conic representation avoids explicit inversion but requires careful handling of near-singular matrices [S1, S3, unofficial].
-*   **Memory:** High VRAM usage: 4GB to view, 12GB to train [S3, unofficial]. Large disk size: 1GB+ for a scene [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Sorting Bottleneck:** Sorting millions of Gaussians is the primary bottleneck. The original implementation uses CUB device radix sort, which is highly optimized but CUDA-specific [S4, unofficial].
+*   **VRAM Usage:** High VRAM usage, approximately 4GB to view and 12GB to train [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Disk Size:** Large disk size, 1GB+ for a scene [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Precision:** Numerical stability in projection and conic computation is critical, especially for degenerate ellipsoids [S1][S3, unofficial].
+*   **Boundary Artifacts:** Chunked training may require boundary support (e.g., Ghost Ellipsoids) to avoid artifacts [S1].
 
